    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green.\n\n## What I did\n\nThe registration-date business-day rule lives in a single tiny helper, `BusinessDay.roll(...)`, used by `BuildOwner`, `RejectDailyLimit`, and `BulkSignup`. To extend weekend-rolling to also skip the five listed public holidays, I confined the entire change to that one class:\n\n- Added a `HOLIDAYS` set (the five pinned dates).\n- Extracted an `isBusinessDay` predicate (weekend **and** holiday check).\n- Replaced the two weekend `if`s in `roll` with a `while (!isBusinessDay) date = date.plusDays(1)` loop, which correctly rolls forward across consecutive non-business days (e.g. a holiday landing on a Friday rolls to the following Monday).\n\n## Keeping structural impact low\n\n- **One file touched**, so the file multiplier is 1.\n- **`roll` stayed simple** \u2014 its cyclomatic complexity actually dropped (two `if`s \u2192 one loop), and `WMC_other` for the class is minimal (just the trivial predicate and private constructor).\n- I put the new logic in a **small cohesive helper** (`isBusinessDay`) rather than growing `roll`, and reused the existing call sites unchanged \u2014 no ripple into `BuildOwner`, `RejectDailyLimit`, or `BulkSignup`.\n\nThe holiday list mirrors the one the tests already replicate in `AcceptanceBase`, and the acceptance test passes.",
